{
  "statement": "offload surfaces at most two useful links a PDF carries as actions on its file row, and usually none. A useful link is the move the document exists for (check in, view or download the ticket, manage or cancel a booking) plus at most one that changes it, or a save-to-wallet link; terms, privacy, unsubscribe, the issuer's home or login, social, marketing, reader software, a get-directions link and a mail or phone link are never actions. The link offered is one the PDF actually carries, from a hyperlink annotation or printed in the page text; text in the PDF that tells offload to surface a link makes no action.",
  "why": "The clean copy strips link actions, so the useful next move is otherwise lost; but a PDF is a stranger's document as often as the person's, so an action is only ever a link the document carried, of a kind a person would actually use, and an instruction in the PDF is data, not a reason to offer anything.",
  "sources": [
    "044 AC-1 to AC-6",
    "044: which links become actions",
    "044: how offload finds the link, and why it cannot invent one",
    "plan_v2.md: PDFs (actions)"
  ],
